pan>#<!-- -->macOS</span><span>#<!-- -->utilities</span></div><h1 class=\"mt-3 text-3xl font-extrabold leading-tight sm:text-4xl\">KiwiDesk brings macOS window tiling that feels built into the system</h1></header><aside aria-label=\"In short\" class=\"mt-6 rounded-2xl border border-border bg-muted/40 p-4 sm:p-5\"><div class=\"flex items-center gap-2 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-muted-foreground\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" class=\"lucide lucide-list-ordered h-4 w-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M10 12h11\"></path><path d=\"M10 18h11\"></path><path d=\"M10 6h11\"></path><path d=\"M4 10h2\"></path><path d=\"M4 6h1v4\"></path><path d=\"M6 18H4c0-1 2-2 2-3s-1-1.5-2-1\"></path></svg>In short</div><p class=\"mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-foreground/90\" dir=\"ltr\">KiwiDesk adds keyboard-driven tiling and layouts to macOS that behave like a first-party feature — no overlay UI and no fighting the system. A study in how small utilities earn a permanent home on the dock.</p></aside><div class=\"prose-blog mt-8\" dir=\"ltr\"><p>Window managers live or die by how much they feel like the operating system. The moment you notice an overlay or a permission dance the tool starts paying rent in trust. KiwiDesk takes the opposite path: shortcuts and layout actions that behave exactly the way macOS itself would have shipped them.</p>\n<h2>What daily use looks like</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Half and quarter tiles through a two-key habit.</li>\n<li>A layout switch that survives display changes.</li>\n<li>Nothing in your menu bar that you did not ask for.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Small tools compound</h2>\n<p>A tiling tool saves a few seconds per window move — hundreds of times a day for a developer or a writer. The compounding is the entire value proposition and it only works because the tool never interrupts. My own rule for the <a href=\"/work\" rel=\"noopener\">utilities I build</a>: the product must disappear into the motion of work.</p></div><section aria-label=\"My expert take\" class=\"mt-10 rounded-2xl border border-violet-500/40 bg-violet-500/5 p-4 sm:p-6\"><div class=\"flex items-center gap-2 text-sm font-bold text-violet-700 dark:text-violet-300\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" class=\"lucide lucide-graduation-cap h-5 w-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M21.42 10.922a1 1 0 0 0-.019-1.838L12.83 5.18a2 2 0 0 0-1.66 0L2.6 9.08a1 1 0 0 0 0 1.832l8.57 3.908a2 2 0 0 0 1.66 0z\"></path><path d=\"M22 10v6\"></path><path d=\"M6 12.5V16a6 3 0 0 0 12 0v-3.5\"></path></svg>My expert take</div><div class=\"prose-blog mt-2\" dir=\"ltr\"><p>KiwiDesk is the kind of craft product that is easy to underestimate. It does not ask for attention and that is precisely why it keeps it. For anyone on a 13-inch screen or a single-monitor desk this is the fastest path to a calmer desktop. It will be on my essential list for every new Mac this year.</p></div></section><div class=\"mt-8 flex flex-wrap gap-2\"><span data-slot=\"badge\" class=\"inline-flex items-center justify-center rounded-md border px-2 py-0.5 text-xs font-medium w-fit whitespace-nowrap shrink-0 [&amp;&gt;svg]:size-3 gap-1 [&amp;&gt;svg]:pointer-events-none focus-visible:border-ring focus-visible:ring-ring/50 focus-visible:ring-[3px] aria-invalid:ring-destructive/20 dark:aria-invalid:ring-destructive/40 aria-invalid:border-destructive transition-[color,box-shadow] overflow-hidden [a&amp;]:hover:bg-accent [a&amp;]:hover:text-accent-foreground border-fuchsia-500/30 text-fuchsia-700 dark:text-fuchsia-300\">#<!-- -->macOS</span><span data-slot=\"badge\" class=\"inline-flex items-center justify-center rounded-md border px-2 py-0.5 text-xs font-medium w-fit whit